# WHY THE SITE IS SLOW, AND WHAT THIS CHANGES.
#
# Every .js and .css here was served "no-cache". That does not mean "do not store" - it means
# nobody may use a copy they already hold without asking the server first. It costs twice:
#
#   1. THE BROWSER re-fetches nexus-core.js on every page. Measured against the live site, that
#      one file is 637KB over the wire and takes ~1.2s. Every page in JAIN-E is a separate HTML
#      document that loads it again, so moving from Accountability to Invoices to CRM pays it
#      each time. In the same page load, chart.js and supabase-js - which ARE cacheable - came
#      back in 9ms from cache while nexus-core.js took 1,358ms.
#
#   2. NETLIFY'S OWN EDGE cannot serve it either. The response header says it plainly:
#         Cache-Status: "Netlify Edge"; fwd=stale; fwd-status=304
#         Age: 0
#      Every request walks back to the origin. That is why this site answers in 0.54-0.94s while
#      cdnjs and jsDelivr - the CDNs it borrows from - answer in 0.15-0.32s from Delhi.
#
# The app already solves this: every script tag carries ?v=<number>, bumped whenever the file
# changes. A URL that changes when its contents change can be cached forever - that is the whole
# point of stamping it. "no-cache" threw that away and left the ?v= doing nothing but adding
# characters.
#
# So the HTML stays no-cache, because the HTML is what carries the new ?v= and must be picked up
# the moment it ships. Everything the HTML points at becomes immutable, because its URL changes
# whenever it does.
#
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ONE RULE THIS DEPENDS ON: bump ?v= in the HTML whenever a .js or .css file changes.
# Ship a change without bumping it and browsers keep the old file for a year. Every page's
# script tags are stamped together, so bumping means a search and replace across *.html, not
# remembering which page uses what.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Images are not versioned, so they get a day rather than a year: long enough to stop them being
# re-fetched on every page, short enough that replacing a logo reaches people the same week.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# THE BROWSER EXTENSION IS PUBLISHED TOO, and its files carry no ?v= at all - popup.js and
# options.js are loaded by name from its own HTML. /tools/* is reachable over HTTP (verified:
# /tools/jaine-automation-extension/popup.js returns 200), so the rules above would otherwise
# freeze them for a year with no way to push a correction. This rule comes last so it wins.
/tools/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
